.TH DRAW 2
.SH NAME
put_header, put_cmd, put_alloc, put_load, put_fill, put_blit, put_free,
put_flush, put_text, atlas_cell, parse_geometry, scan_int, win_name,
win_index, win_dir, win_path \- the draw protocol client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libdraw"
.PP
.nf
.B "ALLOC :: u8(1)"
.B "LOAD :: u8(2)"
.B "FILL :: u8(3)"
.B "BLIT :: u8(4)"
.B "FREE :: u8(5)"
.B "FLUSH :: u8(6)"
.B "HEADER :: 4"
.B "put_alloc :: proc(b: []u8, at: int, id: u32, w: u32, h: u32) -> int"
.B "put_load :: proc(b: []u8, at: int, id: u32, x: u32, y: u32, w: u32, h: u32, pixels: []u8) -> int"
.B "put_fill :: proc(b: []u8, at: int, id: u32, x: u32, y: u32, w: u32, h: u32, color: u32) -> int"
.B "put_blit :: proc(b: []u8, at: int, dst: u32, dx: u32, dy: u32, src: u32, sx: u32, sy: u32, sw: u32, sh: u32) -> int"
.B "put_free :: proc(b: []u8, at: int, id: u32) -> int"
.B "put_flush :: proc(b: []u8, at: int) -> int"
.B "put_text :: proc(b: []u8, at: int, a: Atlas, dst: u32, x: u32, y: u32, text: string) -> (nat: int, put: int)"
.B "Atlas :: struct { first_image_id: u32, per_image: int, cell_w: int, cell_h: int, first_char: u8, count: int }"
.B "atlas_cell :: proc(a: Atlas, idx: int) -> (image: u32, sx: u32)"
.B "parse_geometry :: proc(report: []u8) -> (w: int, h: int, pitch: int, depth: int, ok: bool)"
.B "scan_int :: proc(data: []u8, at: ^int) -> (int, bool)"
.B "win_name :: proc(n: int) -> string"
.B "win_index :: proc(name: string) -> int"
.B "win_dir :: proc(buf: []u8, mnt: string, n: int) -> string"
.B "win_path :: proc(buf: []u8, mnt: string, n: int, leaf: string) -> string"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Libdraw
encodes and decodes the command stream a window's
.B data
file carries, described in
.IR intuition (4).
It writes nothing itself: every
.I put
procedure appends one command to a caller's own buffer at offset
.I at
and returns the offset just past it, or \-1 when the command would not
fit.
A caller collects a batch this way and writes the buffer to
.B data
itself, in pieces no larger than one 9P message if the batch is large;
.IR intuition (4)
answers each write as a whole and fails the entire write on the first
malformed command in it.
.PP
A command is a four-byte header followed by a body of little-endian
.B u32
fields:
.IP
.EX
size[2]   the whole command, header included
verb[1]   one of the six below
zero[1]   reserved, and refused when set
.EE
.PP
.B put_header
writes the header and answers where the body goes;
.B put_cmd
is the shared body for every fixed-field verb, writing the header and then
each of its
.B u32
fields in order.
Both are private to
.IR libdraw ;
they exist to keep the size and offset arithmetic in one place, so a new
verb cannot miscount it, and are documented here because every
.I put
procedure below is built from them.
.PP
The six verbs, and the fields their bodies carry:
.TP 10
.B ALLOC
.IR id ", " w ", " h
.TP
.B LOAD
.IR id ", " x ", " y ", " w ", " h ", then " w * h * 4
bytes of pixels, row by row
.TP
.B FILL
.IR id ", " x ", " y ", " w ", " h ", " color
.TP
.B BLIT
.IR "dst id" ", " dx ", " dy ", " "src id" ", " sx ", " sy ", " sw ", " sh
.TP
.B FREE
.I id
.TP
.B FLUSH
no fields
.PD
.PP
.B Put_alloc
,
.B put_load
,
.B put_fill
,
.B put_blit
,
.B put_free
and
.B put_flush
each write one command of the matching verb.
.I Id
zero means the window's own client area, as both a
.B fill
and a
.B blit
destination; a window is never a valid
.B blit
source.
A pixel is a 32-bit word, and
.B put_load's
.I pixels
argument is already in that row-major layout.
.PP
.B Put_text
packs one
.B blit
per character of
.I text
at
.RI ( x , y )
on
.IR dst ,
stopping at the first character the buffer has no room for.
It answers the new offset and how many characters it consumed; a count
short of the whole string is the caller's cue to write what it has and call
again with the rest, the same rule
.B put_pieces
follows in
.IR chrome (2).
A character outside the
.B Atlas
consumes with no blit, which is why an uploader need not carry a glyph
for space.
.PP
An
.B Atlas
describes glyph images already uploaded with
.BR put_alloc / put_load :
.I count
cells of
.IR cell_w " by " cell_h ,
.I per_image
to a strip, starting at character
.I first_char
and image id
.IR first_image_id .
.B Atlas_cell
answers which image and column cell
.I idx
lives in; a caller baking strips runs the same arithmetic in reverse.
.PP
.B Parse_geometry
reads the four decimal numbers a window's
.B ctl
or
.B /dev/fbctl
answers with: width, height, the row's byte pitch, and the depth.
.B Scan_int
is the digit scanner both that and every other decimal report in this
system are built from: it skips leading space, reads an optional
.B \-
and a run of digits from
.I data
starting at
.IR at^ ,
advances
.I at^
past what it read, and answers false where there was no digit to read.
.PP
.B Win_name
is window
.IR n 's
directory name under a mounted
.BR /srv/draw ,
the decimal string
.IR intuition (4)
also uses; empty past the last window.
.B Win_index
is the inverse, the window number a directory name names, or \-1.
.B Win_dir
builds
.BI mnt / n
into a caller's buffer, the directory a client binds over
.B /dev
to make its window's
.B cons
and
.B mouse
the ones read by name; see
.IR bind (2).
.B Win_path
builds
.BI mnt / n / leaf
the same way.
Both answer the empty string when the buffer is too small.
.SH EXAMPLES
Claim a window, upload a filled square as image 1, and blit it into the
client area:
.IP
.EX
libuser.mount("/srv/draw", "/mnt", 0)
nfd := libuser.open("/mnt/new", abi.O_RDONLY)
n := libuser.read(int(nfd), buf[:])
scan := 0
win, _ := libdraw.scan_int(buf[:n], &scan)

path: [64]u8
fd := libuser.open(libdraw.win_path(path[:], "/mnt", win, "data"), abi.O_WRONLY)

at := libdraw.put_alloc(cmd[:], 0, 1, 16, 16)
at = libdraw.put_fill(cmd[:], at, 1, 0, 0, 16, 16, 0x00ffb028)
at = libdraw.put_blit(cmd[:], at, 0, 8, 8, 1, 0, 0, 16, 16)
at = libdraw.put_flush(cmd[:], at)
libuser.write(int(fd), cmd[:at])
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libdraw/draw.odin
.br
.B sys/libdraw/text.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intuition (4),
.IR chrome (2),
.IR mui (2),
.IR font (2),
.IR fb (3),
.IR bind (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A
.I put
procedure answers \-1, or for
.B put_text
the offset it was given and zero characters consumed, when the command
would not fit in the buffer.
.B Scan_int
and
.B parse_geometry
answer
.I ok
false on text that is not the number or numbers expected; neither reports
which byte was wrong.
Errors a malformed command draws from
.IR intuition (4)
itself are reported over 9P on the write that carried it, not by anything
in this package.
